WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.360
If you're like the estimated 74%

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.640
of adults in the US who use YouTube,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.670
this might be what your
home screen looks like.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:12.640
An algorithmically curated look
into what you like to watch.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.110
Maybe some tips on how
to prepare a new meal

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:17.630
or your favorite vlogger's latest video.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:18.650
But if you're a parent

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:20.500
who shares your phone with your children,

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.470
it probably looks a little more like this.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.090
Kid's content on YouTube is huge.

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:28.560
Views for videos can
reach into the billions.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.720
Five of the top 10 videos
in all of YouTube's history

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.090
are either music videos or kids' videos,

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.576
with Baby Shark having more
views than people on the planet.

00:00:37.576 --> 00:00:39.352
♪ Daddy shark doo doo doo doo doo doo ♪

00:00:39.352 --> 00:00:40.992
♪ Daddy shark ♪

00:00:40.992 --> 00:00:42.410
♪ Grandma shark doo doo ♪

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:47.410
YouTube has created a
revolution, not an evolution,

00:00:48.296 --> 00:00:52.740
in the way kids and families
consume video content.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.740
And that extends then into
how they view toys and play

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:02.460
and all of the associated
things that go with that.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.072
Kids YouTube, for the
unaware, can be adorable.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:08.453
It can be boring.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.685
It can also just be flat out strange.

00:01:15.685 --> 00:01:19.623
♪ Here I am, here I am, how do you do ♪

00:01:19.623 --> 00:01:22.070
But often, it involves toys.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.220
And for toy makers who
embraced the platform,

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.620
it led to record success.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.070
I think L.O.L. Surprise

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.410
unquestionably is the biggest phenomenon

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:32.493
that has ever happened
in the toy industry.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.250
This is the story of how YouTube

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.503
changed kids' toys.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:48.130
Play patterns, the way
children play, is timeless

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.050
but how we deliver on those
play patterns through product

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:54.350
and how we market that to children

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.230
has changed quite a bit over the years.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:01.050
And I would say that the
last five to 10 years

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:05.450
really ranks right up
there with the 1950s,

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.450
when TV commercials directed to children

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.040
was first launched by Mattel.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.590
This is Nancy Zwiers.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.500
She lead the Barbie brand during the '90s

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.880
and spent four years as
the Chief Marketing Officer

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.270
for Spin Master.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:21.200
It used to be that there
would be a few play things

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.370
that parents would bring into the home

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.520
or that children would create themselves

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.270
like a stick and a ball
becomes a play thing.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:31.580
And the industry was at
a certain level of volume

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:32.820
at that point.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.260
Mattel was very innovative

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.550
and actually put the first product on TV

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.399
and advertised it to children.

00:02:41.399 --> 00:02:43.566
Let's play house.

00:02:44.563 --> 00:02:48.500
This created a huge
explosion in the toy industry

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.255
that unfolded across decades.

00:02:51.255 --> 00:02:53.299
♪ The hit of the day when
you're ready to play ♪

00:02:53.299 --> 00:02:55.094
♪ Everyone knows the Slinky ♪

00:02:55.094 --> 00:02:56.921
♪ Here he comes, here he comes ♪

00:02:56.921 --> 00:02:58.809
♪ Greatest toy you've ever seen ♪

00:02:58.809 --> 00:03:02.449
♪ And his name is Mr. Machine ♪

00:03:02.449 --> 00:03:04.423
♪ G.I. Joe, G.I. Joe ♪

00:03:04.423 --> 00:03:06.286
♪ Fighting man from head to toe ♪

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.038
♪ On the land, on the sea ♪

00:03:08.038 --> 00:03:11.100
♪ In the air ♪

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.700
When you go back to the 1980s,

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.810
what do you think of
in terms of hit shows?

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.530
You think of things like
Masters of the Universe,

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:21.480
you think of the original
Transformers and Care Bears.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.180
The joke was they were
22 minute commercials.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.433
Just close your eyes and care.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:31.300
Let's make a TV show
which is really promotion

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.390
for our property or IP to help sell toys.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.470
There were a lot of
those famous characters

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:38.080
in which kids absolutely loved it.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.080
They were totally entertained

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.490
but there were huge toy lines behind them.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:43.890
This is Jim Silver,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.400
a 37 year veteran of the toys industry.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.260
In 1999, he started a
consumer toy review magazine

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:50.530
called Toy Wishes.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:51.960
And now, he's the CEO

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:55.370
of an influencer talent
management group, TTPM.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.600
Regulations changed through the years

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.550
and also parents became aware

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.640
that they were purely commercials.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.560
And you saw a lot of the new channels,

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:05.370
the Disney Channel and Nickelodeon,

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.440
they realized that wouldn't work anymore.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.100
And to them, entertainment
came before licensing.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.530
What happened over time was that

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:18.530
toys started to become designed
for a 30-second commercial.

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:20.630
Closed, it's a mild-mannered tool box.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:22.030
Open, it's a Micro Machines USA.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:23.570
Cruise your mini Micro Machines
vehicles, planes and boats

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:24.450
to the police station, the marina,

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:25.830
the mini motorcycle repair
shop, the gas station,

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:26.663
the construction office.

00:04:26.663 --> 00:04:27.496
Work the real working draw bridge,

00:04:27.496 --> 00:04:28.820
highway, passenger wrap and garage doors.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.780
So if you couldn't explain
it in a 30-second commercial,

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.190
cost reduce it out of the product

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:38.110
and just stick with what you could say.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:43.110
And features of toys, magical
features using electronics,

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:46.140
lights and sounds, and
mechanical movements,

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:49.210
that magic, like they
don't know how it happens,

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.740
that became more and more important.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:53.170
The sound of power is heard.

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:55.338
It's firebolt Batman.

00:04:55.338 --> 00:04:56.860
See ya, Batman.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:58.970
Batman's turbo-powered Batmobile

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:01.170
flies into high gear.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:05.780
Since 2000 with the cable
channels dedicated to kids,

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:09.190
Disney Channel, Nickelodeon,
Cartoon Network,

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:12.260
there became the opportunity to reach kids

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:16.250
in mass numbers every day of the week.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:20.009
And so the toy industries
continued to grow.

00:05:20.009 --> 00:05:21.600
The toy industry for the most part

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.020
was a pretty solid business,

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:24.930
just, you know, growing
a couple percent a year

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:26.200
here and there.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.880
There'd be big, big years here and there

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.810
when there was maybe a hit movie

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:31.120
like a Star Wars movie come out,

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.270
you know, drive a lot of toy sales.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.580
From 2014 to 2019,

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:39.140
the global toy industry would grow by 15%.

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:42.240
At about the same time,
those major cable networks

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.060
of Nickelodeon, Disney
Channel and Cartoon Network

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:47.700
would lose half their total audience.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:51.070
2017 also saw the bankruptcy of Toys R US,

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:52.330
as both parents and kids

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:55.270
discovered and bought more toys online.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:56.660
If you think about kids, they're usually

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:00.180
the first adopters of a new medium.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.210
Kids weren't watching
things like Nickelodeon

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:03.070
as much as they used to.

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:04.963
They were watching things on YouTube.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:09.490
YouTube started in early 2005.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:12.400
And this was like the very early

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.719
web 2.0, this beginning of sort of like

00:06:15.719 --> 00:06:19.190
what at the time was known
as user-generated content.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:20.970
Like web-blogging was really big,

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:23.850
MySpace was the biggest thing
on the internet back then.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:27.050
There was an early video of
a Brazilian soccer legend

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:31.890
doing the stunt tricks that
just exploded on the site,

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.550
one of the first viral videos.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:35.964
Lazy Sunday, if you
remember that SNL short.

00:06:35.964 --> 00:06:39.843
♪ Pass that chronic-what-cles of Narnia ♪

00:06:39.843 --> 00:06:41.699
Ouch, Charlie!

00:06:41.699 --> 00:06:43.590
Oh, Charlie bit me.

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:45.860
If you remember those earliest viral hits.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:50.860
YouTube in the early days was
definitely comedy and music.

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.610
It was a place of experimentation.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:58.320
I was the Chief Audience
Officer at Maker Studios.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.510
I oversaw essentially
the creator partnerships

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:03.750
all over the world.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.540
Maker Studios was one of the largest

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:07.360
multi-channel networks on YouTube

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:09.590
and had partnered with some of
the biggest YouTube channels

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:12.830
of the time, before being
sold to Disney in 2014.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:14.810
Chris is now the CEO of Pocketwatch

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:17.330
which represents some of the
largest kids' stars on YouTube

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:19.640
including Ryan's World and Love, Diana

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:22.430
who can amass over a couple
billion views every month.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:24.160
The truth is one of the very biggest

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.520
and earliest videos on all of YouTube

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.340
was by a creator and star named iJustine

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:34.340
where she unboxed her
first mobile phone bill

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:39.910
for her brand new iPhone,
and it came in a box.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:44.830
And this ultimately morphed
into many different formats

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:46.140
associated with unboxing

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:49.010
and tech was actually really big.

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:53.430
And those became fairly
successful pretty early on

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:54.810
on the platform.

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:58.870
And you saw them expand from tech products

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:03.740
to beauty products, and
then around 2012 and 2013,

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:06.650
we saw this explosion into toys.

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:09.240
So here's the Furby inside right here.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:12.450
We used to call them hands channels, okay?

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:17.360
And a hands channel was
literally a top-down view

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:21.190
of someone's hands
unboxing typically a toy,

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:25.210
and they'd unbox it,
they'd show each component.

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:27.500
We just got Uncle Scrooge.

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:29.460
I would speak to many of those creators.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.210
We signed many of those
creators at Maker Studios

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:34.590
to be our partners.

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:39.590
And it was fascinating in that
it was this whole new genre

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:44.790
and this whole new format
and that corresponded

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:49.790
with a rise of kids and
family viewership on YouTube

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.710
that was stark, that was hard to miss.

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:56.420
And you would see it in the data.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:57.920
And we had access to a lot of data

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.790
at Maker Studios, around YouTube.

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:01.293
Let's see what's inside.

00:09:02.450 --> 00:09:05.690
First up, we have the Stormtrooper Pig.

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:07.390
You saw the launch of EvanTube

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:12.150
who I consider the first child,
so to speak, unboxing star.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:13.780
And they would open toys

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:16.090
and kids were just mesmerized by it.

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:17.740
They loved watching it.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:21.520
And next thing you know, you
had literally 50 unboxings.

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:25.300
They were unboxing just
about every toy, you know?

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:26.560
And people were just really excited.

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:29.580
You'd open the toy and
you'd see a toy pop out

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:32.250
and kids just got excited
seeing it piece by piece.

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:35.440
Yeah, he's in a bathrobe,
he's eating a lobster.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.770
You know, the early days
of these unboxing videos,

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:40.420
the videos were, you know,
from adults' perspective,

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:43.360
just terrible, I mean,
they were 10 minutes long.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:45.900
They were boring, there
was a shaky camera.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:48.920
There was absolutely no production value.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:50.300
And if you think about
these executives, you know,

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:53.710
they're trying to figure out
how to reach this new audience.

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:55.080
And they're brought these videos

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:56.260
and say, "Well, look at this video,

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:00.650
it has 200,000 views on
it, and it's terrible.

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:02.230
When I first heard of it,

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:03.980
I thought it was the weirdest thing.

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:05.760
It just seemed kind of bizarre.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.640
Who would want to watch
someone opening a toy,

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.420
taking it out of the box?

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:12.710
But then when I started thinking about

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.980
this idea of exploration and discovery

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:16.310
as a core play pattern,

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:19.670
the most fundamental
play pattern there is,

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:22.100
it started to make sense.

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:26.147
And I think that I have
to say that the company

00:10:26.147 --> 00:10:31.147
that really jumped on that in
the most concerted way was MGA

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:33.886
with their launch of L.O.L.

00:10:33.886 --> 00:10:36.180
♪ L.O.L. Surprise. ♪

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:40.020
L.O.L. Surprise had three key elements.

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:41.690
Doll play, which is basically

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:44.180
the most popular play pattern there is.

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:47.220
Collectible, so within
each L.O.L. Surprise,

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:48.520
you'd get a little sheet that showed you

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.460
all the other dolls you could collect.

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:51.460
And then the surprise factor.

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:52.900
You didn't know which
doll you were getting.

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:55.190
It was a blind pack as they call it.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:58.110
An L.O.L. was made by
this company called MGA.

00:10:58.110 --> 00:11:01.120
Back then, you know, 2016,
they were a small player

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:05.280
in the toy industry, founded
by this guy named Isaac Larian.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:06.490
He's the CEO and founder.

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:09.410
I have three adult kids and
they were making fun of me

00:11:09.410 --> 00:11:12.870
that, "Oh, you're old, you
don't know anything about

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:14.480
what's happening in today's scene."

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.100
And I said, "Like what?"

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:19.240
And they said, "For example, there is,

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:24.170
do you know about these
iPhone unboxing videos

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:26.670
that get millions and millions of views?"

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:29.243
So I thought they were pulling my leg.

00:11:29.243 --> 00:11:32.970
As children do, to make
fun of their parents.

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:36.458
And then I did go and look one night

00:11:36.458 --> 00:11:39.678
and I was frankly
shocked and flabbergasted

00:11:39.678 --> 00:11:43.450
that why would anybody buy an iPhone

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:48.440
take it home and open the
box and show it on the camera

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.690
and everybody can go watch it?

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:54.840
So I typed toy unboxing, and my God,

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:58.130
I saw so many different videos

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:02.950
and people, kids and adults,
buying toys like an Apple,

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:06.000
and unboxing it on video and posting it.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:07.850
So that gave me an idea

00:12:07.850 --> 00:12:10.363
that why not come up with the ultimate?

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:15.150
And that's the first L.O.L. Surprise.

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:19.445
All right, oh yes, we've got a gold ball.

00:12:19.445 --> 00:12:21.930
L.O.L. changed the way
you opened with toys,

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:23.040
and everybody saw that.

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:25.390
And the packaging became part of the play

00:12:25.390 --> 00:12:27.720
which became part of the entertainment.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:30.320
So normally, if you get a package

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.520
that you just open a package,
and you take the toy out,

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.040
that's not exciting.

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:38.340
You open up L.O.L., you peel
it away, you open up inside,

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:39.700
you have seven surprises.

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:42.100
And it took off like wildfire

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:45.310
and quickly became the
top toy in the country.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:46.920
By December of 2018,

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:48.980
eight of the top 10 toys sold

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:51.470
were all L.O.L. Surprise brand.

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:54.590
And according to MGA, from 2016 to 2020,

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:57.110
L.O.L. would generate over $10 billion

00:12:57.110 --> 00:12:59.330
in retail sales worldwide.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:01.320
L.O.L. was bigger than Star Wars,

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:03.790
it's bigger than Marvel,
it's bigger than Barbie.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:06.730
These brands with huge advertising budgets

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:08.540
are known basically by everyone.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:10.160
You don't have to, you know,

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:12.730
figure out what Marvel or Star Wars is.

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:15.260
And L.O.L. eclipsed them all.

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:16.250
In the toy world,

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:19.480
blind bag or blind box toys
have been growing in popularity.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:22.490
The most famous example in
recent years is Pokemon cards,

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:24.770
where you open packs in
hopes of getting a Charizard,

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:26.840
but more often wind up
with something common

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:28.320
like Gligar here.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.420
Two years before the
launch of L.O.L. in 2014,

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:33.390
the Australian toy company, Moose Toys,

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:36.370
launched a blind bag
collectible called Shopkins.

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:38.660
And in 2016, Spin Master would launch

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:41.623
a more expensive blind box
toy it called Hatchimals.

00:13:46.591 --> 00:13:48.400
But the thing that sets
L.O.L. Surprise apart

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:51.690
is that it mainly focused on YouTube

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:53.160
and not putting ads on YouTube,

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:55.900
but working with creators and influencers

00:13:55.900 --> 00:13:58.180
to get their products in
the hands of these kids

00:13:58.180 --> 00:13:59.810
doing the unboxing videos.

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:02.247
For MGA, actually, the
move towards YouTube

00:14:02.247 --> 00:14:05.660
was kind of a bit lucky and
almost out of necessity.

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:06.710
In late 2015,

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:08.560
Nickelodeon parent company, Viacom,

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:10.690
would sue MGA for breach of contract

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:13.130
after it failed to pay $3.5 million

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:14.730
for the production of a TV series

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:16.980
based on their Lalaloopsy dolls.

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.650
They tried to flex their muscle,

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:22.630
and they said, "Well, if you don't agree

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:27.630
to our way of seeing things
in the conflict that we had,

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:29.700
a separate conflict,

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:34.370
we are going to stop your
advertisement of toys.

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:37.630
At the time, Nickelodeon
was the number one ranked

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:39.550
children's network,

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:42.670
and not to be able to
advertise on Nickelodeon

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:45.210
could have been a death knell,

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:48.883
but MGA, necessity is
the mother of invention,

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:53.990
that caused them to lean
into digital advertising

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:58.780
far more than any big
brand had ever done before

00:14:58.780 --> 00:15:00.180
because they had to.

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:02.840
They had to find a new way to reach kids.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:04.180
The court would ultimately rule

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:07.020
in favor of Viacom, both
in LA federal court,

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:08.830
and on appeal in the Ninth Circuit.

00:15:08.830 --> 00:15:10.110
Larian blamed the court defeat

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:12.500
on what he contends was judicial bias.

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:14.350
Nickelodeon parent company, Viacom,

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:16.880
didn't respond to a request for comment.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:19.467
So that by itself forced us to go and say,

00:15:19.467 --> 00:15:21.890
"Okay, the hell with Nickelodeon.

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:25.190
Let's find another way
to reach to children.

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:27.000
And that's how we went

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:30.550
and doubled up on YouTube, especially,

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:34.250
and the rest, as they say, is history.

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:37.380
So early on, MGA partnered
with YouTube creators

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:40.970
with huge audiences like Cookie Swirl C.

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:42.820
Good morning, Chocolatey Chippy Cookies,

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:44.490
we are gonna be spending the morning

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:47.570
with this L.O.L. Surprise baby, Fancy.

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:50.480
They took it another step further.

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:52.870
They actually helped kids

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:56.101
learn how to do their own unboxing videos.

00:15:56.101 --> 00:16:00.800
They gave them support
and learning and tools,

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:04.010
and so all these kids got to be

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:06.610
kind of a little, mini
influencer themselves

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:09.229
as they were unboxing
their L.O.L. products.

00:16:09.229 --> 00:16:10.380
So just to give you a flavor

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:12.350
of how popular these videos are,

00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:15.370
go onto YouTube and
search L.O.L. Surprise,

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:17.940
and you'll see videos
with a million views,

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:21.750
10 million, 25 million, 50
million, and even 100 million.

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:25.320
There's lots of them, and kids
are watching these videos,

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:28.230
and they're probably watching
them over and over again.

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:30.530
The old way toy
manufacturers would do things

00:16:30.530 --> 00:16:33.800
is they'd buy TV, which was
about 90% of their ad spend.

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:37.310
And then traditional PR, get
on the big morning shows.

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:39.620
It's an entirely different formula.

00:16:39.620 --> 00:16:42.110
It's much harder for
toy companies right now

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:44.270
because the formula is so complicated.

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:47.380
You have to really take a
deep dive into each platform.

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:49.030
Am I promoting on Twitter?

00:16:49.030 --> 00:16:50.750
Am I promoting on Instagram?

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:52.400
Am I promoting on TikTok?

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:54.150
What am I doing on YouTube?

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:57.550
You have to look at every
possible social channel.

00:16:57.550 --> 00:16:59.810
By 2017, big toy brands like Mattel

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:02.360
had shifted nearly 50%
of their marketing budget

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:04.230
to social platform advertising.

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:06.770
Brands would help seed
videos of their toys

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:08.440
and if they were lucky,
that could kick off

00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:10.740
a wave of creators
making their own content

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:11.920
around the product.

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:14.570
In 2020 alone, Tubular Labs estimated

00:17:14.570 --> 00:17:19.570
that L.O.L. related content
had amassed 5.1 billion views.

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:21.730
This idea of imitation.

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:25.320
So when you see kids playing with product,

00:17:25.320 --> 00:17:29.620
it kind of excites the
same neurons in your brain

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:32.110
that happen when you do it yourself.

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:36.690
The more we could show children
having this experience,

00:17:36.690 --> 00:17:40.210
the more kids felt what it
would be like to have it,

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:43.080
and then wanted to
experience it themselves.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:44.100
You know, one of the criticisms

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:46.900
of this whole influencer
movement among kids

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:49.760
is that kids are young,
they don't understand

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:54.290
what's necessarily an
advertisement versus real content.

00:17:54.290 --> 00:17:56.960
And that's a big criticism
of this industry,

00:17:56.960 --> 00:17:59.710
is that a lot of kids are
getting on YouTube at young ages,

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:01.940
not being supervised by their parents

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:04.200
and just watching these videos.

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:06.540
And they don't realize
that maybe that influencer

00:18:06.540 --> 00:18:09.240
is getting paid by the
brand to do that video,

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:11.350
or there's some other compensation.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:13.840
And that, you know,
blurs the lines for kids

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:15.310
as they're sort of developing

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:17.220
and figuring out how to consume media.

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:19.710
Questionable advertising concerns aside,

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:21.750
kids YouTube creators would take off.

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:23.550
Channels like Ryan's World would grow

00:18:23.550 --> 00:18:26.080
to almost 30 million subscribers,

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:28.340
and a whole industry formed around them.

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:31.300
When we first started
working with influencers,

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:35.710
they were very happy to
work for free product.

00:18:35.710 --> 00:18:38.450
You give them the product, and
they'd do something for you.

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:41.150
And gradually, influencers got smarter

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:45.320
and started, you know,
charging for their services

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:47.250
as they started building a following.

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:50.540
Now because it is such a big business,

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:52.390
the dollars can be significant

00:18:52.390 --> 00:18:55.070
investing in big influencers.

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:59.260
When Spin Master launched
one of its new TV series

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:01.210
and associated product,

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:06.210
we made it a point to buy
an advertising package

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:10.550
with Ryan's World to ensure

00:19:10.550 --> 00:19:15.550
that we could get really
quick, big reach right away.

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:19.190
This is an advertisement for Spin Master.

00:19:19.190 --> 00:19:21.090
So from these unboxing videos,

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:23.110
the content's gotten a
lot more sophisticated.

00:19:23.110 --> 00:19:25.670
They're creating
narratives around the toys

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:28.610
with voices and
meta-universes, if you will.

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:31.260
And it's really engaging
content even more so

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:32.920
than the original unboxing
videos for these kids.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:34.900
And the content is coming out maybe

00:19:34.900 --> 00:19:36.390
once a week or even daily.

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:38.040
So if you think about
it, what they've done

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:42.250
is they've created basically
like a TV show around a toy.

00:19:42.250 --> 00:19:45.310
It's not unlike what
we saw back in the '80s

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:47.470
which was criticized
back then, by the way,

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:49.860
when you had toys like G.I. Joe

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:52.130
turn into an animated series,

00:19:52.130 --> 00:19:54.280
or Masters of the
Universe, or Transformers.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:56.510
So we've kind of seen this
full circle now, right?

00:19:56.510 --> 00:19:58.710
So back to where we were content

00:19:58.710 --> 00:20:01.040
and basically, mini television shows

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:03.530
created around toys on YouTube.

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:04.720
Kid's content on YouTube

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:07.570
is likely to face increased
regulation in the coming years.

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:09.310
In 2019, the company would agree

00:20:09.310 --> 00:20:12.580
on a $170 million settlement with the FTC

00:20:12.580 --> 00:20:15.770
for serving ads based on
personal data to kids on YouTube.

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:17.360
YouTube would put more emphasis

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:20.050
behind its made for kids
platform, YouTube Kids,

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:22.000
and each video uploaded to YouTube

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:25.280
must be flagged as either
made for kids or not.

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:27.350
In a statement outlining
changes to the site,

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:28.427
YouTube would say that

00:20:28.427 --> 00:20:31.430
"responsibility is our number
one priority at YouTube,

00:20:31.430 --> 00:20:34.230
and this includes protecting
kids and their privacy."

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:36.190
Still the site is far less regulated

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:37.930
than traditional television.

00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:40.580
I do think parents have
a better understanding

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:44.826
of YouTube now, and the new
genres and formats there

00:20:44.826 --> 00:20:47.320
that they didn't prior.

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:50.770
And they now notice things like

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:53.050
their kid isn't watching a toy unboxing,

00:20:53.050 --> 00:20:54.270
that's a commercial for a toy.

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:57.930
Their kid is watching
other kids play with toys

00:20:57.930 --> 00:20:59.470
in an imaginative way,

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:04.470
which inspires them to go play
with things in the real world

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:06.180
in an imaginative way.

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:07.780
In just a few short years,

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:09.680
YouTube had caused a transfer of power

00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:11.740
from toy and network executives

00:21:11.740 --> 00:21:14.080
to nine-year-olds and their families.

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:17.100
The Kaji family may have
started out as toy reviewers,

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:20.510
but now Ryan has launched
his own line of toys,

00:21:20.510 --> 00:21:21.883
a cartoon series,

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:27.540
he was even a blimp in the 2020
Macy's Thanksgiving parade.

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:30.380
You or I might think
about Spider-Man or Snoopy

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:32.800
but today's kids see that blimp of Ryan

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:34.190
and they relate to him.

00:21:34.190 --> 00:21:36.060
He's the character they
want to grow up with.

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:38.700
He's the character they
want to engage with.

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:40.890
And as we go forward, these
influencers are becoming

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:44.660
more and more like big,
giant global brands.

00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:47.390
And that has profound effects

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:48.990
for entertainment going forward.

